@packages/core/src/graph.sigil from SigilGraphBuilder import { GraphData }

component SigilCompilationBoundary {
  goal {
    Resolve a user-selected compilation seed into the compilation boundary that
    completely covers the affected semantic scope, so pointing at changed work
    yields a safe target instead of assuming the selector is the right unit.

    Keep boundary selection deterministic and explainable from loaded workspace
    facts alone, so every host reaches the same target for the same workspace.
  }

  interface {
    BoundarySelection {
      selectCompilationBoundary(resolvedWorkspace, seed, options) returns the
      requested scope, the resolved target, and the selection evidence that relates
      them.

      seed is exactly workspace, component, file, location, or directory; each
      identifies affected semantic evidence rather than a final target.

      the resolved target is exactly workspace, file, or component.

      options.exactTarget skips boundary inference. It preserves workspace, file,
      and component seeds as targets, resolves a location seed to its containing
      file, and rejects a directory seed.

      selection reports the strategy, the affected, covered, and uncovered semantic
      units, the deciding tie-break when one applied, and a reason when no boundary
      was inferred.

      strategy is exactly exact-target, covering-component, or workspace-fallback.

      diagnostics reports an invalid or unresolvable seed; a caller refuses the run
      rather than treating the reported workspace target as a real choice.
    }

    SeedValidation {
      A seed is validated against the loaded workspace before any scope is derived.

      An empty selector, an absolute path, and a path containing a parent segment are
      invalid.

      A component name that matches no loaded component, a file that is not a loaded
      source, and a directory with no loaded source beneath it are unresolvable.

      An invalid or unresolvable seed yields an error diagnostic and no inferred
      boundary.
    }

    AffectedScope {
      A seed resolves to the set of affected component identities and affected source
      files.

      A component seed contributes that component and its declaration file; an
      ambiguous name without a declaration path contributes every component that
      carries it.

      A file seed contributes the file plus every component declared in it.

      A location seed contributes the innermost component enclosing that
      authoritative source position, and otherwise falls back to its file.

      A directory seed contributes every loaded file beneath the normalized
      workspace-relative path, including declarationless files, plus their declared
      components.
    }

    BoundaryClosure {
      A candidate covers the affected scope only when its closure contains every
      affected component identity and every affected file.

      An ordinary _module.sigil component has no special closure or ranking
      privilege.

      A component candidate closes over its own declaration, directly selected Tag
      providers and their required owner context, and actual direct consumer uses.
    }

    ProximityRanking {
      Candidates are ordered by the segment distance from every affected directory to
      the candidate directory.

      Each candidate yields one distance vector sorted greatest-to-least, and the
      lexicographically smallest vector wins.

      Equal vectors are separated by normalized repository-relative path, then by
      exact-case component name.
    }
  }

  logic {
    BoundarySelection {
      Return the workspace immediately for a workspace seed, because it is already
      the widest boundary.

      For an exact target request, preserve a workspace, file, or component seed;
      select the containing file for a location seed. Record that inference was
      skipped. Reject a directory seed because it is not a compilable target.

      Prefer a covering component; otherwise fall back to the workspace.
    }

    AffectedScope {
      Normalize every path to a workspace-relative path before comparison, so
      selection does not depend on how the caller spelled the seed.

      Collect components and files into sets, so a repeated contribution cannot
      change the result.
    }

    BoundaryClosure {
      Traverse required structural owner and direct-use relationships with visited
      identities so cycles terminate; do not infer a namespace from directories.

      Absorb each reached component declaration path into the closure.
    }

    ProximityRanking {
      Derive the affected directory list from the affected files, sort it, and
      compute one distance vector per candidate.

      Report the rule that separated the winner from the closest rival rather than
      the last comparison performed while sorting.
    }

    WorkspaceFallback {
      Fall back to the workspace when the seed resolves to no loaded Facet, and when
      no candidate covers the complete affected scope.

      Record the reason for the fallback in the selection evidence.
    }

    SeedValidation {
      Validate the seed before deriving any affected scope, so an unresolvable
      selector never reaches candidate ranking.

      Report the offending path or name on the diagnostic so a host can correct the
      invocation.
    }
  }

  constraints {
    BoundarySelection {
      Selection uses only loaded configured-workspace facts and GraphData carrying
      the resolved Tag-selection and consumer-use graph.

      Selection performs no filesystem discovery and does not depend on discovery
      order, evaluator behavior, package manifests, or agent judgment.

      The resolved target either covers the complete affected scope or is the
      workspace; a partial component target is never selected silently.

      The requested scope and the resolved target remain distinct values in the
      result, so a host can always show what it asked for beside what will be
      compiled.
    }

    AffectedScope {
      A directory seed selects loaded files only; an unloaded or excluded path
      contributes nothing.

      A trailing separator on a directory seed does not change its meaning.
    }

    SeedValidation {
      A reported workspace target alongside an error diagnostic is not a selection;
      compiling it would silently widen an invocation the caller got wrong.

      Validation depends only on the loaded workspace, so the same selector is valid
      or invalid identically across hosts.
    }

    BoundaryClosure {
      Coverage is exact set containment; a candidate that reaches most of the
      affected scope does not cover it.
    }

    ProximityRanking {
      Ranking is total and deterministic, so equal inputs always produce the same
      winner regardless of candidate order.
    }
  }

  decisions {
    SeedsRatherThanExactTargets {
      Decision: Treat component, file, location, and directory selectors as
      affected-scope seeds and infer the final compilation boundary from the graph,
      with an explicit exact-target opt-out.

      Scope: Governs normal compilation target resolution for every host; excludes
      evaluator behavior, profile selection, and report rendering.

      Assumptions: A selector marks where work happened, which is not reliably the
      unit that owns the full contract and its relationships.

      Trade-offs: A selected component may compile a wider boundary than the caller
      named, so the result must always explain which boundary was chosen and why.

      Design issues addressed: Selecting a leaf component proves nothing about
      whether that component is the right validation unit, so every host previously
      had to reimplement the same selection policy in prompts or scripts.

      Discarded alternatives: Leaving the policy in host instructions was rejected
      because it cannot be tested, versioned, or shared across hosts. Inferring
      silently without an opt-out was rejected because a deliberately narrow run is a
      legitimate need.

      Consequences: A user or agent can point at what changed and receive the same
      explainable boundary from every host.

      Revisit when: Selection needs evidence the resolved graph does not carry.
    }

    CompleteCoverageOrWorkspace {
      Decision: Select a candidate only when it completely covers the affected scope,
      and otherwise compile the workspace.

      Scope: Governs the fallback rule; excludes how the affected scope itself is
      derived.

      Design issues addressed: A partial boundary silently omits affected semantic
      units from validation, which is worse than compiling more.

      Discarded alternatives: Selecting the best partial candidate was rejected
      because it hides uncovered evidence behind a plausible result.

      Consequences: An unrelated multi-directory selection resolves to the workspace
      and reports why.
    }

    RejectUnresolvableSeeds {
      Decision: Validate the seed against the loaded workspace first and report an
      error diagnostic, rather than letting an unresolvable selector fall through to
      workspace compilation.

      Scope: Governs selector validation; excludes the workspace fallback used when a
      valid seed has no covering boundary.

      Design issues addressed: A mistyped component name or an excluded path would
      otherwise resolve to an empty affected scope and compile the entire workspace,
      which is both expensive and silently not what was asked for.

      Discarded alternatives: Treating an unresolvable selector as an empty scope was
      rejected because the widest possible action is the worst default for an
      invocation error.

      Consequences: A host distinguishes a deliberate workspace compilation from a
      rejected invocation, and can surface the offending name or path.
    }

    GraphDerivedClosure {
      Decision: Compute the coverage closure from the resolved Tag-selection and
      consumer-use graph rather than by invoking purpose retrieval per candidate.

      Scope: Governs closure construction inside boundary selection; excludes the
      retrieval results supplied to evaluators.

      Assumptions: The resolved graph already carries the provider, consumer, and
      ownership relationships that retrieval derives its closure from.

      Trade-offs: Closure construction is duplicated logic that must stay aligned
      with retrieval as relationships evolve.

      Design issues addressed: Ranking many candidates through an asynchronous
      retrieval call would make selection slow and would couple a pure ordering
      decision to retrieval's evidence-shaping concerns.

      Consequences: Selection stays synchronous, side-effect free, and callable from
      a language server without awaiting retrieval.

      Revisit when: Retrieval exposes a reusable synchronous closure projection.
    }
  }

  cases {
    BoundarySelection {
      A leaf component resolves to the nearest covering component when its closure
      covers every affected identity and file.

      A component resolves to itself when it is the smallest complete boundary.

      An exact-target request over the same component preserves the component as the
      final target.

      An exact-target request at a location inside a component selects its file
      without inferring a broader boundary.

      An exact-target request over a directory is rejected rather than widened to the
      workspace.
    }

    AffectedScope {
      A location inside an operational contract selects its owning component and can
      then select a broader covering boundary.

      A directory holding several related contracts resolves to one deterministic
      boundary.

      Two components sharing a name are contributed together when no declaration path
      disambiguates them.
    }

    ProximityRanking {
      Two covering components are separated by distance vector, then normalized
      declaration path and exact component name.
    }

    WorkspaceFallback {
      A directory holding unrelated contracts with no common covering boundary
      compiles the workspace and reports the reason.
    }

    SeedValidation {
      A misspelled component name is rejected rather than silently compiling the
      whole workspace.

      A directory outside the workspace root and an empty selector are rejected as
      invalid paths.

      An excluded or unloaded file is rejected as unresolvable.
    }
  }
}
